#!/usr/bin/env -S python3 -I
"""Notice when the library's background work stalls (run by whisipedia-stall.timer every 10 minutes).

2026-10-19: the American Stories ingest held the ingest lock for 10.5 hours and a dozen download lanes
sat waiting behind it, and the Chronicling America lane stopped on HTTP 429 at 05:13; both were found
by hand hours later. This looks for such things and writes each one once, when it starts and when it
ends, to ``<state>/watch/events.log`` (and the journal), where a Claude session (a Monitor tailing the
log) sees it at once. The serious ones (PUSHED: a unit failed or down, the API silent, memory stalls,
jobs owed a start, a silent index build, the disk or the NAS) are also raised as ``watch:<key>``
alerts through ``wsp alerts raise``/``resolve``, so they reach the owner: a notification when
critical, the morning digest and ``wsp://system`` (2026-11-05 deep dive: 83 conditions in 14 days, a
failed nightly index and five API outages among them, reached nobody without a Claude session).

* ``unit-failed:<unit>``      a whisipedia unit failed; a long-running service (RUNNING: the API, the
                              orchestrator, the trawler, the memory guard) not running for
                              WSP_STALL_UNIT_DOWN_MIN minutes (20), unless tools/wsp-yield stopped it for
                              memory (``jobs-owed`` says so): 2026-11-05, the memory guard had been
                              stopped by a halt for 6 days and nothing said so. The unit's last journal
                              lines are saved at once to ``<state>/watch/failures/`` and the message
                              names that file (the journal keeps only about 14 hours on this machine:
                              deploy/journald/), and the unit's run log when it keeps one
* ``job-idle:<unit>``         a background job (whisipedia-job-*) used under IDLE_CPU_S of CPU in the
                              last WSP_STALL_IDLE_H hours (default 3): stuck, or waiting on something
                              (the message names a long-held lock, or a frozen background slice); not
                              the jobs named by WSP_STALL_IDLE_OK (default whisipedia-job-*-watch.service).
                              A job seen waiting its turn (a ticket in a lock's queue, under
                              WSP_STALL_QUEUE_IDLE_H), waiting for another lane's source lock, or
                              pausing between rounds is not idle, nor for IDLE_H after it was last seen
                              so: its quiet hours were the wait, not a hang (2026-11-08/09: 43 alerts
                              "may be waiting on the ingest lock" were lanes minutes out of the queue)
* ``lock-held:<lock>``        a lock (ingest, heavy, index...) held by one process over WSP_STALL_LOCK_H
                              hours (8), counted from the first run that saw it held (the scrub lock
                              is the orchestrator's for its whole life, and is not counted). The nightly
                              ``index build`` holds index and heavy for WSP_STALL_INDEX_LOCK_H (14) before
                              it counts, as one condition (2026-11-05 deep dive: most normal builds take
                              8 to 10 hours, and every one raised the pair at 8)
* ``index-silent``            the running ``index build`` has written nothing to its run log
                              (``<state>/logs/index-build/*-<pid>.jsonl``) for WSP_STALL_INDEX_SILENT_H
                              hours (6; a normal build's longest quiet step is under 5): hung, which the
                              lock's age cannot tell (2026-11-02 and 11-03: builds that stopped writing
                              hours before they died looked like healthy ones)
* ``jobs-owed``               jobs tools/wsp-yield stopped for memory (``jobs/yield-stopped``) not started
                              again after WSP_STALL_OWED_MIN minutes (30): 2026-10-22, every lane stood
                              still for 10 hours and nothing here said so
* ``job-down:<name>``         a job named in ``jobs.conf`` (the jobs resumed after a reboot) not running
                              for WSP_STALL_DOWN_H hours (1): stopped, crashed, or finished (then take it
                              out of jobs.conf)
* ``api-down``                /v1/health did not answer 200 twice in a row
* ``halted``                  tools/wsp-shutdown left its marker (``halted`` in tools/wsp-bg's state) and
                              none of the long-running services runs: their stop and the silent API
                              are this halt's, and raise no alert (2026-11-05: halts for a game are
                              routine)
* ``files-high:<unit>``       a core service holds over 80% of the files it may open (the API ran out at
                              1,024 on 2026-10-20 and answered pages with HTTP 500)
* ``mem-stall:<unit>``         the background slice, the download lanes' slice or the API spent over
                              WSP_STALL_PRESSURE_PCT (50) of the last 5 minutes wholly stalled on memory
                              (memory.pressure "full avg300"): its ceiling is too low for what runs in
                              it, and everything in it crawls (the lanes, 81% on 2026-10-20, unnoticed:
                              each still used enough CPU not to look idle)
* ``disk-low:<mount>``        under WSP_STALL_MIN_FREE_GB free locally (50) or on the NAS (2000)
* ``nas-missing:<mount>``     the NAS share is not mounted (its free space is not checked then)
* ``log``                     a new problem line in a lane log (HTTP 429, Traceback, FAILED, a fetch
                              that failed (exits 7 and 8 are complete; 5, an earlier fetch waiting for
                              its ingest, is the lane's routine), partitions left for later, no space);
                              one note per problem and log a day, never the backlog. The retries a lane
                              makes on its own (fetch exits 4 and 6, items left for a later run) are
                              noted once a week: they were 70% of the notes, the same waiting outage
                              every day, and lane-run.sh raises its own ``lane:`` alert once its tries
                              run out (2026-11-05 deep dive)
* ``systemd-slow``            systemctl did not answer within WSP_STALL_SYSTEMCTL_S seconds (15), or the
                              run's budget ran out: the checks that need it were skipped this run

Conditions are re-checked every run: ``RAISED`` when one starts, ``CLEARED`` when it is gone. A check
that could not run clears nothing. The whole run keeps within WSP_STALL_BUDGET_S seconds (240), under
the unit's TimeoutStartSec=5min (2026-11-04 trawl: each systemctl call could wait 60 s, ~26 of them a
run under memory pressure, and a run killed at 5 minutes wrote nothing, exactly when memory was short).
``--status`` prints what is open now. WSP_STALL_STATE, WSP_STALL_LOGS (colon-separated globs),
WSP_STALL_LOCKS, WSP_STALL_PROC, WSP_STALL_CGROUP_ROOT, WSP_STALL_HEALTH_URL, WSP_STALL_ALERT_CMD (the
``wsp`` that raises the alerts; default this checkout's .venv/bin/wsp, run from the checkout, which
reads WSP_CONFIG_DIR) and WSP_STALL_NOW are for tests.
"""

